Subject: Grow the property array by doubling

What this does
  Changes the growth of an object's property array from a fixed increment to a
  doubling of its capacity.

Why we need it
  A fixed increment reallocates once per property added, so an object that
  accumulates many properties reallocates a linear number of times.

What it adds
  Reallocation follows a geometric schedule and the excess capacity is dropped by
  the existing compaction path when the object settles. Small objects keep the same
  initial capacity, and property indices, hash links and shape identity behave as
  before. No opcode is added or retired and BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -7141,7 +7141,11 @@ static no_inline int resize_properties(JSContext *ctx, JSShape **psh,
     intptr_t h;
 
     sh = *psh;
-    new_size = max_int(count, sh->prop_size * 3 / 2);
+    /* Amortised doubling (upstream grows by 3/2). Objects built one field at a
+       time reallocate their property array half as often, an object that never
+       exceeds two properties pays nothing, and prop_size stays equal to the
+       shape's power-of-two hash_size. */
+    new_size = max_int(count, sh->prop_size * 2);
     /* Reallocate prop array first to avoid crash or size inconsistency
        in case of memory allocation failure */
     if (p) {
